## Analysis of Prior Data

| Exp | bath_temp | pullcast_speed | coupon_wait | nips_wait | Elastic Modulus | Strain@50bar | CV |
|-----|-----------|---------------|-------------|-----------|-----------------|--------------|-----|
| 1 | 5°C | 10 | 30s | 125s | 199.9 | 0.328 | 0.065 |
| 2 | 5°C | 15 | 30s | 1200s | 226.8 | 0.280 | 0.048 |
| 3 | 5°C | 20 | 30s | 1800s | 204.8 | 0.461 | 0.064 |
| 4 | 5°C | 12 | 15s | 1800s | 171.4 | 0.455 | 0.082 |
| 5 | 10°C | 15 | 30s | 1200s | 229.2 | 0.305 | 0.085 |
| 6 | 5°C | 17 | 30s | 1200s | 257.2 | 0.409 | 0.089 |
| 7 | **15°C** | 15 | 30s | 1200s | **355.3** | **0.360** | **0.044** |

**Key observations:**
- **Bath temperature is the dominant driver**: 5°C→10°C→15°C at similar other conditions shows a clear monotonic increase in Elastic Modulus (226→229→355 MPa). Warmer bath = higher modulus.
- **Strain at 50 bar**: Lower is better; Exp 2 (5°C, 15mm/s, 1200s) gave the lowest strain (0.280), but Exp 7 (15°C) gave 0.360 — a trade-off exists.
- **CV**: Exp 7 has the best CV (0.044), indicating high reproducibility.
- **Casting speed ~15 mm/s** with **30s N₂ purge** and **1200s bath** appears consistently good.
- The next logical step is to push bath temperature higher (toward 20–25°C) to continue the modulus trend, while keeping other parameters at the best-performing configuration.
- **nips_bath_wait_time**: 1200s performed well; longer (1800s) did not help and increased strain.
- **coupon_to_bath_wait_time**: 30s N₂ consistently outperforms 15s.

**Next experiment**: Increase bath_temp to **20°C** (continuing the upward trend), keep pullcast_speed=15, coupon_wait=30s, nips_wait=1200s, nitrogen=True, polymer_wt=17.0 (max, so mixing_temp=25°C).

